  "worked_solution": "First, we note that $z=0$ is an excluded solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nWe multiply both sides of the equation by $2z,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{z+1}{2z} & =2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z} \\\\ 2z\\cdot (\\frac{z+1}{2z}) & =2z\\cdot (2-\\frac{2z+1}{2z}) \\\\ 2z\\cdot \\frac{z+1}{2z} & =2z\\cdot 2-2z\\cdot \\frac{2z+1}{2z} \\\\ \\cancel{2z}\\cdot \\frac{z+1}{\\cancel{2z}} & =2z\\cdot 2-\\cancel{2z}\\cdot \\frac{2z+1}{\\cancel{2z}} \\\\ z+1 & =4z-(2z+1) \\\\ z+1 & =4z-2z-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $z,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+1 & =4z-2z-1 \\\\ z+1 & =2z-1 \\\\ -z & =-2 \\\\ z & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $z=0$ as a solution. But since $z=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $z=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
